  "summary": "Recent preprints place mitochondrial carriers and dynamics regulators at the center of tissue-specific metabolic control in muscle, liver, adipose and macrophages.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is that mitochondrial substrate entry and organelle remodeling set tissue metabolic output. Two items identify SLC25A35 as the mitochondrial carrier for branched-chain keto acids (BCKAs), with MCT1 and MCT2 mediating cellular entry; one reports that import constrains hepatic BCKA handling, and another links BCKA uptake to adipocyte lipogenesis. MTCH2 is reported to support metabolic remodeling in muscle satellite cells, including differentiation, respiration and fatty acid oxidation. In myeloid cells, mDIC deletion shifts adipose macrophages toward an efferocytosis-associated state while preserving mitochondrial respiration. In KPC cachexia mice, muscle OPA1 overexpression or BNIP3 knockdown partly preserved mass and function, sex-dependently. The newest element is the transporter-level account of BCKA import; the remaining items extend mitochondrial remodeling to other cell types and disease states.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-20"
}
